{"sounds":{"intro":"Tatar (Kazan Tatar) is a Turkic language written in Cyrillic. It has six extra letters, and its vowels follow harmony: front vowels (ә, е, э, и, ө, ү) go with front-vowel endings, back vowels (а, ы, о, у) with back-vowel endings.","rows":[["ә","ae, like \"a\" in \"cat\"","әни (ae-NI)"],["ө","like French \"eu\" or German \"ö\"","өч (euch)"],["ү","like German \"ü\"","күз (kyuz)"],["җ","j, like \"judge\"","җылы (jy-LY)"],["ң","ng, like \"sing\"","мең (meng)"],["һ","h, breathy","һава (ha-VA)"],["ы","like \"i\" in \"bit\" said further back","ык (yk)"],["х","kh, like Scottish \"loch\"","хәзер (khae-ZER)"],["ч","ch (soft, between ch and sh)","чәй (chaey)"],["ш","sh","шәһәр (shae-HAER)"]],"notes":["Stress usually falls on the last syllable. Tatar has no gender and no word for \"the\". The verb comes last.","The say column uses simple English-style spelling. \"ae\" is the sound of \"a\" in \"cat\", \"ö\" and \"ü\" are as in German."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["zero","нуль","nool"],["one","бер","ber"],["two","ике","i-KE"],["three","өч","euch"],["four","дүрт","dyurt"],["five","биш","bish"],["six","алты","al-TY"],["seven","җиде","ji-DE"],["eight","сигез","si-GEZ"],["nine","тугыз","tu-GYZ"],["ten","ун","oon"]]},{"id":"11-to-20","title":"11 to 20","items":[["eleven","ун бер","oon ber"],["twelve","ун ике","oon i-KE"],["thirteen","ун өч","oon euch"],["fourteen","ун дүрт","oon dyurt"],["fifteen","ун биш","oon bish"],["sixteen","ун алты","oon al-TY"],["seventeen","ун җиде","oon ji-DE"],["eighteen","ун сигез","oon si-GEZ"],["nineteen","ун тугыз","oon tu-GYZ"],["twenty","егерме","ye-ger-ME"]]},{"id":"tens-and-big-numbers","title":"Tens and big numbers","items":[["twenty-one","егерме бер","ye-ger-ME ber"],["thirty","утыз","u-TYZ"],["thirty-one","утыз бер","u-TYZ ber"],["forty","кырык","ky-RYK"],["fifty","илле","il-LE"],["sixty","алтмыш","alt-MYSH"],["seventy","җитмеш","jit-MESH"],["eighty","сиксән","sik-SAEN"],["ninety","туксан","tuk-SAN